<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Togashi Masachika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1455">1455</a>–<a href="/years/1488">1488</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Togashi Masachika was the military governor of Kaga province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. He won control of the province with the help of True Pure Land believers but then turned against them. In 1488 a league of believers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikko-ikki</a>, rose against him, besieged his castle and drove him to suicide, after which the leagues ruled Kaga for nearly a century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Masachika belonged to the Togashi family, which held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaga-province">Kaga Province</a> as shugo in the Muromachi period. Kaga's warriors resisted his rule, and low-level rural violence was probably common there. When the struggle between Hosokawa Katsumoto and Yamana Sōzen grew into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a> in 1467, the unrest in Kaga became a civil war within the clan over the post of shugo: Masachika sided with the Hosokawa, and his younger brother Kochiyo with the Yamana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kochiyo drove Masachika out of Kaga in 1473. Kochiyo patronised the Takada school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodo-shinshu">Jōdo Shinshū</a> and persecuted followers of its rival, the Hongan-ji, so Masachika turned to Asakura Toshikaga, lord of Echizen, and to the priests of Yoshizaki who followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennyo">Rennyo</a>, head of the Hongan-ji. He promised the ikki that he would relieve their poverty. With Toshikaga's troops and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikkō-ikki</a> rising across Kaga, he quickly overthrew his brother.</p><p>The alliance soon broke down. In 1474 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikko-ikki">Ikkō-ikki</a>, saying he had not kept his promises, rebelled without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennyo">Rennyo</a>'s approval and were defeated, many fleeing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etchu-province">Etchū Province</a>. In 1475 Shimotsuma Rensu falsely claimed Rennyo's backing for a new rising, which was also put down, and Rennyo excommunicated him. Unrest continued, and the believers refused taxes and seized revenue and land. In 1487 Masachika marched to help Shogun Ashikaga Yoshihisa against Rokkaku Tokoyori in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his absence three of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennyo">Rennyo</a>'s sons, Rengo, Renkō and Rensei, raised a revolt joined by between one hundred thousand and two hundred thousand people. Masachika hurried back and at first defeated the ikki armies, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> families including the Motoori and Yamagawa joined the rebels, who cut him off from allies in Echizen, Etchū and Noto. Besieged in his burning castle, he committed seppuku. The rebel vassals put forward his uncle and former shugo, Yasutaka, as a figurehead. The historian David L. Davis suggests the revolt arose because Masachika was trying to end the general anarchy of Kaga and impose discipline on the province.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Togashi Masachika” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1488 a league of believers rose against the governor <b>Togashi Masachika</b>, besieged his castle and drove him to suicide.</blockquote><a href="/state/kaga-ikko-ikki">Kaga Ikko-ikki</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Tako Castle. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
